    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-16430",
      "problem": "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$ Given that $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.005.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989.$",
